<script>
' Persistent chrome for every "/browse/*" route — mounted once by the root
' FlashTheaterRouterOutlet, and stays mounted across every navigation between its own children
' (HomeScreen/ScheduleScreen, below), since neither of those changes THIS component's own matched
' route entry ("browse") in the app's route tree (see MainScene.thr's setRouting()). Verify live
' that Shell's own node identity is unchanged (via queryAppUi) across a "/browse" ->
' "/browse/schedule" navigation — the actual proof this is working, not just that it compiles. See
' GRAMMAR.md's "Router" section and findings/router.md.
'
' The sidebar (below) is reserved for NAVIGATION ONLY — every element in it is either the menu
' itself or a small diagnostic readout about navigation/focus state (interceptorUrl/Status,
' focusStateReadout, menuHint). `FavoriteCounter` used to live here too (moved to HomeScreen.thr,
' 2026-08-19) — it's ordinary routed CONTENT, not navigation chrome, and its own diagnostic label
' stack never actually fit the sidebar's own width/height budget cleanly (see
' findings/router-transitions.md's own writeup of that whole saga). Its own on:key[options] handler
' stays here regardless — see that function's own doc comment below for why a NAVIGATION-only
' component still owns the key handler for a piece of content it no longer renders.
'
' ROUTER-OUTLET TRANSITIONS, demonstrated on `childOutlet` itself (below): navigate-out:/
' navigate-in:/back-out:/back-in: slide `childOutlet`'s own translation off/on screen around every
' swap (see the slideOutLeft/slideInFromRight/slideOutRight/slideInFromLeft animation declarations
' below), and loadingComponent holds RouterLoadingSpinner up until whichever screen just mounted
' calls router.markReady() or loadingTimeout elapses. Verify live: every menu press slides the
' content pane left-then-in-from-right; the physical back key slides it the reverse way. Every
' screen under this outlet now calls router.markReady() — most synchronously from their own
' setup() (the spinner never actually appears for these; the outlet reveals as soon as setup()
' returns), EXCEPT RouterTransitionDemo.thr, which deliberately defers markReady() behind a real
' GetPosts.thr fetch (the spinner genuinely shows there, for as long as the request takes). See
' GRAMMAR.md's "Router" section and findings/router-transitions.md.
'
' THE VACUUM RULE, demonstrated end to end. This menu is the canonical TV layout: a persistent
' side menu beside content that swaps as you move through it. Both menu entries navigate, and
' focus MUST stay in the menu the whole time — a framework that handed focus to each freshly
' mounted screen would yank focus out from under the user on every single menu move, which is
' exactly the behavior the vacuum rule exists to prevent: an automatically-chosen focus target is
' applied only when nothing currently holds focus. Here focus lives on a menu item that is NOT
' inside the swapped subtree, so it survives every navigation untouched, and each mounted screen's
' own default-focus="true" element only takes effect when focus actually enters that screen (press
' "right" from the menu). Verify live: press Down/Up in the menu and confirm the highlight stays on
' the menu items while the content beside it changes.
'
' Two focusable elements of its own, which used to be impossible here — an earlier design had the
' enclosing outlet call focusComponent() on whatever it mounted, so persistent chrome with its own
' focusable content would have had focus stolen back from the nested outlet's own, earlier focus
' decision. The pending-target mechanism (deepest proposal wins, applied only into a vacuum)
' removed that restriction entirely. See findings/router.md.
'
' `divider` is a thin, non-focusable line between the sidebar (0-380) and the content pane
' (400-1280) — purely visual, makes the two regions unmistakable on screen. `childOutlet` itself is
' offset by [400, 0] once, here — the single point where the sidebar's own width is known — so every
' mounted screen's own template can use simple local coordinates (0,0 = top-left of the content
' pane) without needing to know the sidebar exists at all.
'
' Every size/position below is `scale`d, authored for this app's configured design resolution (HD
' — see flash-theater.config.json; this whole app's layout was originally built against a
' 1280x720 canvas, confirmed by every root element's own literal 1280/720 dimensions, so HD is
' this app's real baseline, not FHD). At FHD the whole sidebar+content layout scales UP by 1.5x to
' fill the larger screen, rather than sitting shrunk/confined in the top-left corner of an
' unfilled canvas — confirmed live, see findings/scale.md.
'
' "options" (the * button) bumps the shared favorite counter — reachable from EITHER HomeScreen or
' ScheduleScreen, since neither declares its own on:key[options] handler: real Roku key bubbling
' routes an unhandled key up through each SceneGraph ancestor's own onKeyEvent in turn
' (HomeScreen/ScheduleScreen's own generated onKeyEvent first, then Shell's, since Shell is their
' real SceneGraph parent via the outlet) — exactly the same cross-component bubbling this app's
' focus system already relies on elsewhere, now demonstrated across a router-mounted boundary too.
' Writes straight to the shared `store(favoriteCount)` value (see bumpFavorite() below) rather than
' reaching into a specific FavoriteCounter instance via callFunc — deliberately, since FavoriteCounter
' now lives on HomeScreen.thr, not here, and is torn down/recreated every time HomeScreen itself is
' (leaving Home for Schedule and back, say). The store is the actual persistent source of truth;
' pressing "options" from Schedule (where no FavoriteCounter is even mounted) still correctly bumps
' the count, visible the next time HomeScreen mounts and reads it back reactively.
'
' `lastInterceptedUrl`/`lastInterceptedStatus` prove taskManager.onRequestSent(...)/
' onResponseReceived(...) (see GRAMMAR.md's "Task manager" section) actually work CROSS-COMPONENT:
' registered once here, from Shell's own automatic router-mounted setup() call, they update no
' matter which DIFFERENT screen's own request Task (GetPosts/GetPost/CreatePost, reached via
' RequestDemoScreen) actually fires. Placed in the persistent sidebar specifically so the readout
' stays visible regardless of which screen is currently mounted in childOutlet — the whole point of
' a GLOBAL, register-once interceptor is that it isn't tied to whichever screen happened to issue
' the request.
' Deliberately NOT bound to "right": an unconditional component-level on:key[right] here would
' swallow the arrow key before the focus system's own LRUD fallthrough ever ran, making it
' impossible to walk right out of the menu into the mounted content — confirmed live while building
' the menu above. A component-level (non-focus-gated) handler always shadows LRUD navigation for
' whatever key it claims, so claim a key the focus system doesn't use.
' isFocused / isInFocusChain — reserved, reactive focus-state fields the compiler synthesizes for
' any component that reads them (and for no other component, so nothing here costs anything in a
' component that doesn't care). This is the clearest possible place to see the difference between
' the two: Shell OWNS the menu but only WRAPS the mounted screen, so
'   - focus on a menu item      -> isFocused = true,  isInFocusChain = true
'   - focus inside HomeScreen   -> isFocused = false, isInFocusChain = true
'   - (Shell is always in the chain in this app, since everything visible lives under it)
' Both are ordinary fields, so they compose with `derived` and with {#if} exactly like any other
' field — no new syntax, no observers to wire by hand. See GRAMMAR.md's "Focus system".
scale field rootWidth: integer = 1280
scale field rootHeight: integer = 720
scale field menuItemWidth: integer = 300
' Stays 50 (56px row pitch, below) even though FavoriteCounter no longer lives in this sidebar at
' all (moved to HomeScreen.thr, 2026-08-19 — see this file's own top comment): `menuHomeTranslation`/
' `menuScheduleTranslation` below must stay Y-aligned with HomeScreen.thr's own `prompt` and
' ScheduleScreen.thr's own `backPrompt`/`lateDefault` (cross-axis overlap is what lets "right" from
' either menu row reach straight into that screen's own content — see runtime-assets/FocusManager's
' `bestCandidate()`), so this sizing can't be freely changed without updating those two screens'
' own translations to match — deliberately left alone here, out of scope for the FavoriteCounter
' move itself.
scale field menuItemHeight: integer = 50
scale field dividerWidth: integer = 2
scale field childOutletWidth: integer = 880
scale field childOutletHeight: integer = 720

scale state menuHomeTranslation: object = [40, 190]
scale state menuScheduleTranslation: object = [40, 246]
scale state menuLoadingTranslation: object = [40, 302]
scale state menuCardsTranslation: object = [40, 358]
scale state menuTasksTranslation: object = [40, 414]
scale state menuStreamsTranslation: object = [40, 470]
scale state menuRequestsTranslation: object = [40, 526]
scale state menuTransitionDemoTranslation: object = [40, 582]
scale state menuLabelInset: object = [16, 12]
scale state dividerTranslation: object = [380, 0]
scale state childOutletTranslation: object = [400, 0]
' Below every menu row (which ends at 582 + menuItemHeight 50 = 632).
scale state interceptorUrlTranslation: object = [40, 642]
scale state interceptorStatusTranslation: object = [40, 664]
scale state focusReadoutTranslation: object = [40, 686]
scale state menuHintTranslation: object = [40, 706]

' Router-outlet transition demo — see GRAMMAR.md's "Router" section and
' findings/router-transitions.md. `childOutlet` is wrapped in `childOutletViewport` (see the
' template below) — a plain `Rectangle` with `clippingRect` set to its own bounds. This is the
' EXACT scroll-viewport pattern GRAMMAR.md's "Scroll-into-view" section already documents ("the
' field-declaring component's own template root element must NOT itself be the thing that
' translates — only content nested further inside it should move, so the root element's own bounds
' stay a stable reference window throughout"): `childOutletViewport` never itself moves and clips
' everything inside it to its own 880x720 window, so `childOutlet`'s own slide (below) is confined
' to that window instead of visibly sliding across the sidebar — confirmed live this is NOT
' automatic (Roku never clips a node's children to its own bounds by default) and NOT something
' FlashTheaterRouterOutlet handles for you; an author wanting the slide confined to its own region
' has to add this wrapper themselves, same as any other scrollable/clipped region in this DSL.
'
' Because `childOutletViewport` (not `childOutlet` itself) now carries the FIXED absolute position
' (`childOutletTranslation`, [400, 0]), `childOutlet`'s own resting translation, relative to ITS
' OWN parent, is genuinely `[0, 0]` — every "resting" keyframe below reflects that. (An earlier cut
' of this demo had `childOutlet` positioned directly at [400, 0] with no wrapper, which needed
' [400, 0] as the resting keyframe instead — got wrong twice live, once via an unscaled offset and
' once via using [0, 0] instead of [400, 0] as the true rest position; adding the wrapper here
' removes that whole class of mistake by making [0, 0] correct again.)
'
' `scaled: true` (object form — the array shorthand doesn't support it) is still required:
' `childOutletViewport`'s own `width="880"` (below) is `scale`d, so at any factor besides 1.0 the
' REAL on-screen window no longer matches a literal 880px slide offset — the exact footgun the
' built-in `fly`/`slide` presets already set `scaled: true` on their own pixel offset to avoid.
animation slideOutLeft {
  target: childOutlet
  duration: 0.25
  easeFunction: "outQuad"
  translation: { keyValue: [[0, 0], [-880, 0]], scaled: true }
}
animation slideInFromRight {
  target: childOutlet
  duration: 0.25
  easeFunction: "outQuad"
  translation: { keyValue: [[880, 0], [0, 0]], scaled: true }
}
animation slideOutRight {
  target: childOutlet
  duration: 0.25
  easeFunction: "outQuad"
  translation: { keyValue: [[0, 0], [880, 0]], scaled: true }
}
animation slideInFromLeft {
  target: childOutlet
  duration: 0.25
  easeFunction: "outQuad"
  translation: { keyValue: [[-880, 0], [0, 0]], scaled: true }
}

derived focusStateLabel: string = describeFocus(isFocused, isInFocusChain)
derived childOutletClipRect: object = [0, 0, childOutletWidth, childOutletHeight]
' `store(...)` is write-only syntax (`store(<key>) = <expr>`, a statement form) — reading the
' current value back needs a `watch` binding instead, same as any other store consumer. Reactive
' (not `read`, a one-time snapshot) since Shell stays mounted for the whole "/browse/*" subtree and
' must always bump the CURRENT count, not whatever it happened to be when Shell first mounted.
watch favoriteCount = store(favoriteCount)

state lastInterceptedUrl: string = "onRequestSent: (none yet)"
state lastInterceptedStatus: string = "onResponseReceived: (none yet)"

private function describeFocus(focused: boolean, inChain: boolean): string {
  if (focused) {
    return "focus: MENU"
  }
  if (inChain) {
    return "focus: CONTENT"
  }
  return "focus: elsewhere"
}

' Writes straight to the shared store — see this file's own top comment and the "options" key
' comment above for why: FavoriteCounter (HomeScreen.thr) reads this reactively wherever/whenever
' it happens to be mounted, so this works correctly even when pressed from a screen that isn't
' rendering it at all right now.
private function bumpFavorite(key: string, press: boolean) {
  if (press) {
    store(favoriteCount) = favoriteCount + 1
  }
}

' Each menu entry navigates WITHOUT taking focus with it — see this file's own top comment.
private function showHome(key: string, press: boolean) {
  if (press) {
    router.navigate("/browse")
  }
}

private function showSchedule(key: string, press: boolean) {
  if (press) {
    router.navigate("/browse/schedule", { day: "Mon" })
  }
}

private function showLoadingDemo(key: string, press: boolean) {
  if (press) {
    router.navigate("/browse/loading")
  }
}

private function showCards(key: string, press: boolean) {
  if (press) {
    router.navigate("/browse/cards")
  }
}

private function showTaskDemo(key: string, press: boolean) {
  if (press) {
    router.navigate("/browse/tasks")
  }
}

private function showStreamDemo(key: string, press: boolean) {
  if (press) {
    router.navigate("/browse/streams")
  }
}

private function showRequestDemo(key: string, press: boolean) {
  if (press) {
    router.navigate("/browse/requests")
  }
}

private function showTransitionDemo(key: string, press: boolean) {
  if (press) {
    router.navigate("/browse/transition-demo")
  }
}

' Global HTTP request/response interceptors, registered ONCE, here — see GRAMMAR.md's "Task
' manager" section. `req`/`resp` are the resolved-options AA and the RAW ft_httpFetch response
' respectively (never `result`/`error`, which are each request component's own
' parseResponse/parseError-transformed output) — fired for EVERY request Http {} component's own
' traffic anywhere in the app, not just ones Shell itself creates.
private function onAnyRequestSent(req: dynamic) {
  state lastInterceptedUrl = "onRequestSent: " + req.url
}

private function onAnyResponseReceived(resp: dynamic) {
  state lastInterceptedStatus = "onResponseReceived: HTTP " + resp.httpStatusCode.ToStr()
  if (not resp.parseSucceeded) {
    state lastInterceptedStatus = lastInterceptedStatus + " (parse error: " + resp.parseErrorMessage + ")"
  }
}

' Router-mounted components get an automatic setup() call the moment they're mounted (see
' GRAMMAR.md's "Router" section) — Shell is mounted once, for the whole "/browse/*" subtree, so
' this runs exactly once regardless of how many child screens the user navigates through.
public function setup() {
  taskManager.onRequestSent(onAnyRequestSent)
  taskManager.onResponseReceived(onAnyResponseReceived)
}
</script>

<component on:key[options]="{bumpFavorite()}">
<Rectangle id="root" color="0x0A0A0AFF" width="{rootWidth}" height="{rootHeight}">
  <Label id="interceptorUrlReadout" text="{lastInterceptedUrl}" color="0x66CCFFFF" translation="{interceptorUrlTranslation}" />
  <Label id="interceptorStatusReadout" text="{lastInterceptedStatus}" color="0x66CCFFFF" translation="{interceptorStatusTranslation}" />
  <Rectangle id="menuHome" focusable="true" color="0x2A2A2AFF" width="{menuItemWidth}" height="{menuItemHeight}" translation="{menuHomeTranslation}" on:key[OK]="{showHome()}">
    <Label id="menuHomeLabel" text="Home" color="0xFFFFFFFF" translation="{menuLabelInset}" />
  </Rectangle>
  <Rectangle id="menuSchedule" focusable="true" color="0x2A2A2AFF" width="{menuItemWidth}" height="{menuItemHeight}" translation="{menuScheduleTranslation}" on:key[OK]="{showSchedule()}">
    <Label id="menuScheduleLabel" text="Schedule" color="0xFFFFFFFF" translation="{menuLabelInset}" />
  </Rectangle>
  <Rectangle id="menuLoading" focusable="true" color="0x2A2A2AFF" width="{menuItemWidth}" height="{menuItemHeight}" translation="{menuLoadingTranslation}" on:key[OK]="{showLoadingDemo()}">
    <Label id="menuLoadingLabel" text="Loading demo" color="0xFFFFFFFF" translation="{menuLabelInset}" />
  </Rectangle>
  <Rectangle id="menuCards" focusable="true" color="0x2A2A2AFF" width="{menuItemWidth}" height="{menuItemHeight}" translation="{menuCardsTranslation}" on:key[OK]="{showCards()}">
    <Label id="menuCardsLabel" text="Cards demo" color="0xFFFFFFFF" translation="{menuLabelInset}" />
  </Rectangle>
  <Rectangle id="menuTasks" focusable="true" color="0x2A2A2AFF" width="{menuItemWidth}" height="{menuItemHeight}" translation="{menuTasksTranslation}" on:key[OK]="{showTaskDemo()}">
    <Label id="menuTasksLabel" text="Task demo" color="0xFFFFFFFF" translation="{menuLabelInset}" />
  </Rectangle>
  <Rectangle id="menuStreams" focusable="true" color="0x2A2A2AFF" width="{menuItemWidth}" height="{menuItemHeight}" translation="{menuStreamsTranslation}" on:key[OK]="{showStreamDemo()}">
    <Label id="menuStreamsLabel" text="Stream demo" color="0xFFFFFFFF" translation="{menuLabelInset}" />
  </Rectangle>
  <Rectangle id="menuRequests" focusable="true" color="0x2A2A2AFF" width="{menuItemWidth}" height="{menuItemHeight}" translation="{menuRequestsTranslation}" on:key[OK]="{showRequestDemo()}">
    <Label id="menuRequestsLabel" text="Request demo" color="0xFFFFFFFF" translation="{menuLabelInset}" />
  </Rectangle>
  <Rectangle id="menuTransitionDemo" focusable="true" color="0x2A2A2AFF" width="{menuItemWidth}" height="{menuItemHeight}" translation="{menuTransitionDemoTranslation}" on:key[OK]="{showTransitionDemo()}">
    <Label id="menuTransitionDemoLabel" text="Transition demo" color="0xFFFFFFFF" translation="{menuLabelInset}" />
  </Rectangle>
  <Label id="focusStateReadout" text="{focusStateLabel}" color="0x66FF99FF" translation="{focusReadoutTranslation}" />
  {#if isFocused}
    <Label id="menuHintLabel" text="press RIGHT to enter content" color="0x888888FF" translation="{menuHintTranslation}" />
  {/if}
  <Rectangle id="divider" color="0x2A2A2AFF" width="{dividerWidth}" height="{rootHeight}" translation="{dividerTranslation}" />
  <!-- The clipping viewport — see the animation declarations' own doc comment above. Never itself
       translated; its own fixed position (childOutletTranslation) is what used to sit directly on
       childOutlet before this wrapper existed. clippingRect confines everything inside (the outlet
       and whatever it slides) to this Rectangle's own 880x720 window, so a mid-slide screen never
       visibly renders over the sidebar to its left. color is fully transparent — this Rectangle
       exists purely as a clip boundary, not a visible fill. -->
  <Rectangle id="childOutletViewport" color="0x00000000" width="{childOutletWidth}" height="{childOutletHeight}" translation="{childOutletTranslation}" clippingRect="{childOutletClipRect}">
    <!-- navigate-out:/navigate-in: play on router.navigate(...) (every menu press above);
         back-out:/back-in: play on router.back() (the physical back key) — reversed pair, see the
         animation declarations above. No ="" on any of the four — a bare attribute (no value at
         all) means the same as ="" here, same as it does for Layer 2's own transition:/in:/out:
         (see GRAMMAR.md's "Template" section) — there's never an override to pass for a custom
         animation reference like these four, so the ="" would be pure ceremony. width/height mirror
         the viewport's own (also used to center loadingComponent — see
         FlashTheaterRouterOutlet.brs's own _showSpinner). loadingComponent holds every screen's own
         reveal behind RouterLoadingSpinner until that screen calls router.markReady() — every
         screen under this outlet now does so (see each screen's own setup()), so loadingTimeout
         below is a rarely-hit safety net, not the common path; only RouterTransitionDemo.thr
         deliberately delays its own markReady() behind a real fetch. -->
    <FlashTheaterRouterOutlet
      id="childOutlet"
      width="{childOutletWidth}" height="{childOutletHeight}"
      navigate-out:slideOutLeft navigate-in:slideInFromRight
      back-out:slideOutRight back-in:slideInFromLeft
      loadingComponent="RouterLoadingSpinner" loadingMinDuration="0.15" loadingTimeout="3"
    />
  </Rectangle>
</Rectangle>
</component>
